 { Vladimir Akopian - Alexandr Schimanov, Plovdiv 2012 Learning objective: This game is an example of White's chances of a mating attack. } 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Ng5 Ngf6 6. Bd3 e6 7. N1f3 Bd6 8. Qe2 h6 9. Ne4 Nxe4 10. Qxe4 Qc7 11. O-O Nf6 $6 ( { An imprecision. Theory suggests } 11... b6 12. Qg4 Kf8 13. b3 Bb7 14. Bb2 Nf6 15. Qh3 Nd5 { with an approximately balanced game. PH: Note that } Kf8 { is necessary because both } O-O $2 13. Bxh6 { and } g5 13. Qh3 { threatening Bxg5 are both not acceptable for Black. It looks weird, but the king would be relatively safe on f8, while not giving White much of a focus for counterplay. } ) 
12. Qh4 b6 13. Bg5 $14 { White has a better position due to his lead in development, extra space and attacking prospects against Black's uncastled king. } ( { The inaccurate } 13. Re1 Bb7 14. Ne5 ( 14. Bg5 $5 ) 
 { is met by } O-O-O $11 { when Black's king is safe on the queenside. } ) 
Be7 ( 13... Nd5 { is no better, due to } 14. c4 Nb4 15. Bb1 $14 { when Black's knight on b4 is doing nothing. } ) 
14. Bf4 ( { After } 14. Rad1 $5 { Black can trade off bishops with } Nd5 15. Bxe7 Qxe7 16. Qg3 O-O 17. c4 Nf6 18. Rfe1 $14 { but White maintains a preferable position due to his space advantage. } ) 
Qd8 $2 { Inaccurate; Black does not quite realize how dangerous White's threats really are. } ( 14... Nd5 $1 { is necessary: } 15. Bxc7 Bxh4 { But here White has several options to maintain a small advantage, for example: } 16. Be5 $5 ( { A worthy alternative is } 15... Qd8 15. Bxb6 Bxf2 17. Rxf2 axb6 18. Ne5 f6 $1 19. Ng6 $14 ) 
Bf6 17. Rfd1 { Black cannot easily release his position, for example } Bb7 { hoping for ...c6-c5 next move: } 18. c4 Ne7 19. c5 $5 { ⩲ Keeping Black's light-squared bishop out of the game for a while. } ) 
15. Ne5 $16 { A few precise moves have been enough for White to dominate the board; Black is already on the verge of losing. } Bb7 ( { It is too late for simplification by } 15... Nd5 $2 16. Qg4 Nxf4 17. Qxf4 O-O 18. Nxc6 { as Black simply loses a pawn. } ) 
 ( { A better defence is } 15... Nd7 16. Qg3 Nxe5 ( 16... O-O $4 17. Bxh6 $18 ) 
17. dxe5 ( { Or } 16... Bb7 16. Bxe5 Bf6 18. Rae1 $16 { but Black can hold on for longer. } ) 
Kf8 18. Rad1 $16 { and White is much better, but he does not have an obvious route to victory. } ) 
16. Rad1 { The black monarch is trapped in the centre, thus placing rooks on the d- and e-files is simply logical. } Bd6 $6 { This is probably Black's last inaccuracy. } ( { Black cannot escape the centre with } 16... O-O $2 { since } 17. Bxh6 $1 { just wins, for example: } gxh6 18. Qxh6 { The threat is Ng4 followed by Nxf6 and mate on h7. } Re8 19. Bg6 $1 fxg6 ( { Or } 19... Rf8 20. Rd3 { and mate is unstoppable. } ) 
20. Qxg6 Kh8 21. Nf7 ) 
 ( { It is not too late for } 16... Nd7 17. Qg3 Nxe5 18. dxe5 g5 $5 { when Black gets to survive longer, though White is indisputably better. } ) 
17. Rfe1 Qe7 ( { Again } 17... O-O $2 { loses to } 18. Bxh6 gxh6 19. Qxh6 { followed by Re3-g3. } ) 
 ( { An alternative is } 17... Bxe5 18. Bxe5 O-O { though after } 19. Qh3 $1 { the threat of Re3-g3 wins. } ) 
18. Qh3 $1 ( { Creating the nasty threat of } 19. Ng6 $1 fxg6 20. Rxe6 { . } ) 
Nd5 19. Bg3 O-O ( { Trying to escape to the queenside with } 19... O-O-O { fails to: } 20. Ng6 $1 fxg6 ( 20... Qc7 21. Nxh8 $18 ) 
21. Rxe6 Qd7 22. Bxd6 $18 ) 
 ( 19... Nf6 20. Ng6 $1 fxg6 21. Rxe6 { and wins. } ) 
20. c4 { Black's position is desperate. } Nf6 ( 20... Bxe5 { doesn't help as after } 21. Bxe5 Nf6 22. Re3 { the entrance of the rook into the attack decides the game: } Rfd8 23. Rf3 Nd7 24. Bf4 { Threatening Bxh6. } f5 25. Rg3 Kh7 26. Re1 $1 { Followed by Bxf5 or Rxe6 winning. } ) 
 ( { Neither does } 20... Nb4 { achieve anything: } 21. Bb1 { and Black is defenceless, for example } Rfe8 22. a3 Na6 23. c5 Bc7 24. Bh4 f6 25. Bg6 { with a crushing attack. } ) 
21. Bh4 $18 { Threatening Ng4 followed by Bxf6 winning. } g5 22. Bxg5 hxg5 23. Qh6 { Black resigns since mate cannot be avoided without great material loss. } ( Z0 { For example: } 23. Qh6 Rfd8 24. Ng4 $1 Nxg4 25. Qh7 Kf8 26. Qh8 ) 
 { Final remarks 1. White's attack became irresistible after Black fell behind in development. The sacrifice Bxh6 is a recurring idea in this structure. 2. Black's best attempt for a defence was playing ...Nd7xe5 hoping White would play dxe5. In many variations, such a change of structure would block many lines of attack allowing Black to survive longer. } 
 1-0

