 { Bartlomiej Macieja - Jesus Nogueiras Santiago, Puebla 2013 Learning objective: This game is an example of how White may slowly improve his position as long as Black cannot achieve the breaks ...c6-c5 or ...e6-e5. } 1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. Nf3 dxe5 5. Nxe5 c6 6. h3 $5 { This move is rather uncommon, but the position is quite flexible and there are many options to maintain an opening edge. The virtue of this move is to prevent Black from trading his light-squared bishop with ...Bg4xf3 when the knight retreats from e5. } ( { More common is } 6. Be2 Nd7 7. Nf3 $14 { with a comfortable position. } ) 
g6 7. Bc4 Bg7 8. O-O O-O ( 8... Bxe5 9. dxe5 O-O 10. Bh6 Re8 11. Qd2 $14 ) 
9. Re1 Be6 { Strange-looking, but Black does not really have better squares for this bishop. } ( { Normal would be } 9... Nd7 10. Nf3 e6 11. Bf1 { when White stands better as Black cannot easily release his position with either ...c6-c5 or ...e6-e5, for example: } c5 $6 12. c4 Nb4 13. Bg5 Qb6 14. dxc5 Nxc5 15. Nc3 $14 { Black is struggling to finish developing his pieces; play might continue: } Bd7 $6 16. Be7 Rfe8 $2 17. Bxc5 $18 ) 
 ( { The alternative } 9... Bf5 { faces tactical problems after } 10. Nc3 $1 Nxc3 11. bxc3 { when Black is under strong pressure since } Nd7 $2 { fails to } 12. Nxf7 $1 Rxf7 13. g4 { winning a piece. } ) 
10. Bf1 Nd7 11. Nf3 { To retain his space advantage White must avoid trading pieces if possible. } ( { Erroneous would be } 11. Nxd7 $6 Qxd7 12. c3 Qc7 { when Black easily equalizes after } 13. Nd2 Rad8 14. Nf3 c5 ) 
Nc7 { Black probably would like to proceed with ...Bd5 followed by ...Bxf3 alleviating his space problem. } ( { An interesting alternative is } 11... b5 $5 { preventing White from gaining more space with c2-c4: } 12. c3 Qc7 13. Nbd2 h6 { White's position is only a little better. } ) 
12. Bg5 $14 { White has good control of the position. } Re8 13. Nbd2 ( { Preventing ...Bd5xf3 with } 13. c4 { allows Black some counterplay with } b5 $1 { fighting for control of the d5-square. } ) 
Bd5 14. c4 Bxf3 15. Nxf3 { Black finally manages to trade off one piece, but all his remaining pieces are awkwardly placed. } Ne6 ( { The alternative } 15... h6 16. Be3 e5 $1 { would alleviate some of Black's space problems. } ) 
 ( { However } 15... h6 { can be met by } 16. Bh4 $1 { preventing ...e7-e5, and maintaining the pressure. } ) 
16. Be3 { Macieja enjoys a lasting advantage due to his space and the bishop pair. He will proceed to consolidate in the centre, in order to embark on kingside operations. } Nc7 $6 { Placing the knights in this way is rather unnatural, and only makes White's task simpler. } ( { A healthier move is } 16... Qc7 17. Qc2 Nf6 18. Rad1 Rad8 { though Black's position is definitely inferior. } ) 
17. Qb3 Qc8 18. Rad1 $16 { White simply controls the game, and he may soon start a kingside initiative with h3-h4-h5. } a5 ( { The break } 18... e5 $6 { is refuted by } 19. dxe5 Nxe5 20. Ng5 $16 { followed by Ne4-d6. } ) 
19. g3 { Preparing h3-h4 next move. } ( { It might be too soon for } 19. h4 { because after } e5 20. Nxe5 Nxe5 21. dxe5 Bxe5 22. g3 $14 { White preserves an advantage due to his bishops, but h3-h4 is not as useful in this position. } ) 
e6 ( { Probably better is } 19... e5 { releasing Black's position somewhat. } 20. Nxe5 Nxe5 21. dxe5 Rxe5 22. Re2 $16 { followed by Red2 with firm control of the d-file. } ) 
20. h4 $1 { The typical h4-h5 plan is applied and once again Black must choose between allowing this plan or preventing it with ...h7-h5. In either case, White will gain good tactical opportunities against Black's castled position. } a4 21. Qc2 h5 ( { It is not too late to try breaking up White's centre with } 21... e5 { though now White has the extra option: } 22. d5 $5 Na6 23. dxc6 bxc6 24. Bh3 $16 { Black's position is very difficult, but this is better than being asphyxiated by White's pieces. } ) 
 ( { Instead if } 21... Nf6 22. Bg5 h6 23. Bf4 $16 { Black does not have any useful moves, While White can slowly improve his position with Ne5 and Bd3 followed by potential sacrifices on g6 or f7. } ) 
22. Bf4 { Ruling out the possibility of an ...e6-e5 break, as well as taking control of the d6-square; a possible plan is Ng5-e4-d6. } Rd8 23. Bd3 { The threats against Black's kingside begin to appear. } Nf8 ( { For example, the careless } 23... Ne8 { allows a potential (but not necessary) sacrifice: } 24. Bxg6 $5 fxg6 25. Qxg6 Nf8 { The only move } ( { instead, the natural } 25... Ndf6 $2 { loses to } 26. Ng5 Qd7 27. Rxe6 { followed by Rde1 and Re7. } ) 
26. Qxh5 Nf6 27. Qg5 $16 { White has a dangerous attack. } ) 
24. Bg5 Re8 ( { The rook is awkwardly placed after } 24... Rd7 25. Be4 Ne8 26. Ne5 $1 { since } Bxe5 27. dxe5 $16 { leaves Black in a precarious position. } ) 
25. a3 { Fixing the weak a4-pawn. White's position is ideal; he has a free hand to prepare decisive action on the kingside. } b5 $6 { Black decides to search for counterplay by fighting for the d5-square. Unfortunately for him, it is too late and this only makes White's job easier. } ( { If } 25... Nd7 { White could break through the defence with } 26. Ne5 ( { Or } 25... b5 25. Bxg6 $5 fxg6 27. Qxg6 $16 ) 
Nf8 27. Bf4 Rd8 28. Be4 Ne8 29. c5 $5 { ± followed by Nc4. This plan is analyzed in the next game. } ) 
26. Rc1 { Simple and effective - the c6-pawn is now a serious weakness. } bxc4 ( { If } 26... Qa6 27. Be4 Rec8 28. Ne5 $1 Bxe5 29. dxe5 $16 { Black's kingside is worse than ever, the threat being Bf6 followed by Qd2-h6-g7#. } ) 
27. Qxc4 Nb5 28. Ne5 $18 { Black's position simply collapses. } Na7 29. Be3 Rd8 30. Be4 { Black resigns as he cannot prevent the loss of pawns on c6 and a4. Final remarks 1. White's spatial advantage played a major role throughout the game. The reader should notice how Macieja avoided trading pieces when possible, for example with 11. Nf3. 2. Black was constantly forced to calculate White's speculative threats, such as 23. Bd3 followed by a potential 24. Bxg6 sacrifice. It is possible Macieja did not even waste a minute calculating such a sacrifice, though Black was obliged to assess whether the sacrifice worked, or otherwise prevent it just to be safe. 3. Black's desperate attempt with ...b7-b5 accelerated the downfall of his position, but the loss was imminent anyway, and White had great flexibility in choosing which winning plan to implement. } 
 1-0

