 { Ivan Ivanisevic - Pero Ascic, Zupanja 2007 Learning objective: This game is an example of the advance c4-c5, in order to gain control of the d6-square and to fix the centre, also preventing Black from releasing his position with ...c6-c5. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c6 ( { The Catalan main line is } 3... Nf6 4. g3 Be7 5. Bg2 O-O 6. O-O dxc4 { when Black should have a perfectly playable position. } ) 
4. g3 Nd7 5. Bg2 Bd6 6. O-O Ngf6 7. Nc3 O-O { Black has developed his pieces schematically with little regard to specific move orders. In particular, we should note that Black's bishop is hanging on d6, hence the reply: } 8. Nd2 $1 { Simply logical, and creating difficulties for Black. White will play e3-e4 next move and recapture with Nxe4 gaining a crucial tempo. } Re8 ( { In the event of } 8... Bc7 9. e4 dxc4 ( { Or } 9... dxe4 10. Ndxe4 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 { since } e5 $2 { is met by } 12. d5 $1 { ± with a strong passed pawn. } ) 
10. e5 Nd5 11. Nxc4 $14 { White is better. This is similar to the fragment of Andreikin-Kramnik which will be analyzed in the next chapter. } ) 
9. e4 dxe4 10. Ndxe4 Nxe4 ( { After } 10... Bc7 11. Re1 h6 12. f4 Nxe4 13. Nxe4 Rb8 14. Be3 a5 15. Qc2 b6 16. Rad1 $14 { as in the game Papaioannou - Lajthajm, Budva 2009, White dominates the board since neither ... e6-e5 nor ...c6-c5 is possible. } ) 
11. Nxe4 Be7 $6 { Giving up the b8-h2 diagonal and admitting 6... Bd6 was imprecise. } ( { A healthier continuation is } 11... Bc7 { preventing } 12. Bf4 { , though after12. Bg5 f6 13.Be3⩲ White enjoys a pleasant spatial advantage. } ) 
12. Bf4 $1 { The best move, keeping an eye on the ...e6-e5 break. } ( { Instead, the premature } 12. c5 $6 { would allow } e5 $1 { after which Black solves most of his problems, for example } 13. Be3 exd4 14. Bxd4 Nf6 15. Bxf6 Qxd1 16. Rfxd1 Bxf6 17. Nxf6 gxf6 { with an inferior but defensible position. } ) 
Nf6 ( { Black is not well prepared for } 12... c5 $2 { since after } 13. dxc5 Nxc5 14. Nd6 $1 { White wins the pair of bishops and the queenside majority will advance easily, for example: } Bxd6 15. Bxd6 Qb6 16. Qd4 Nd7 17. c5 Qa6 18. a4 $1 { and White wins after either } Nf6 19. b4 { or } e5 19. Rfe1 ) 
13. c5 $16 { As a young chess player I was very puzzled by moves like this. My strategy books had taught me that c4-c5 was a colossal positional mistake since the d4-pawn would be permanently vulnerable as a backward pawn, and the d5-square would be easily controlled by Black's knight. This is all true, but in this pawn structure the c4-c5 move has far more benefits than drawbacks. This move fixes the central structure, permanently banning the ...c6-c5 break and locking in Black's light-squared bishop. In addition, the d6-square becomes accessible to a knight, and clearly White's knight on d6 would be far more powerful than Black's knight on d5. Finally, White secures a stable spatial advantage, and the d4-pawn is easily defended in the current position, though it could become vulnerable if White did not proceed correctly. } Nd5 14. Bd6 { By trading dark-squared bishops White takes possession of the d6-square. } b6 { This move does not help to create counterplay, but it does turn the c6-pawn into a new target. } ( { If } 14... Bxd6 15. Nxd6 Re7 16. Re1 { White is in full control, and possibly the best continuation is: } Nf6 17. Qd2 Rd7 18. Rad1 Rxd6 ( { Conserving material by } 18... Ne8 { leads to } 19. Nc4 Qc7 20. b4 Nf6 21. a4 Rd8 22. b5 $16 { - a typical plan to create pressure against the c6-pawn; White is close to winning. } ) 
19. cxd6 Qxd6 20. Qa5 $16 { Black is unlikely to survive an exchange down. } ) 
15. Qa4 $1 b5 $6 { This move is basically accepting a lost position. The inclusion of the moves 14... b6 and 15... b5 only turned the c6-pawn into a chronic weakness. } ( { Slightly better is } 15... Qd7 { keeping the tension, though after } 16. Rac1 Rd8 17. Bxe7 Nxe7 18. Nd6 { only White is able to create threats, for example } a5 19. Rfe1 Rb8 20. Qc2 Ba6 21. Bh3 $1 { ± Threatening Nxf7 followed by Bxe6. } ) 
16. Qc2 a5 17. Rfe1 Bf8 18. Bxf8 Rxf8 19. Nd6 { White's advantage is obvious; we just need to compare knights and bishops. White can trade his bishop for Black's knight anytime, while Black cannot make much use of his locked-in light-squared bishop. } Rb8 ( 19... Ne7 $2 { loses to } 20. Nxb5 $1 ) 
20. Be4 h6 ( { If } 20... g6 { then White is likely to proceed with } 21. Qd2 Ne7 22. h4 $1 { followed by h4-h5-hxg6 with a powerful attack. } ) 
21. Qd2 Bd7 ( { More stubborn is } 21... Ne7 { though White progresses without difficulty, for example } 22. Qf4 Bd7 23. h4 Qc7 24. h5 Nc8 25. Rac1 Ne7 26. Qe5 { Threatening Nxf7. } f6 27. Qf4 Nc8 28. Bd3 $5 { ± White threatens Qe4-h7. } ) 
22. Bxd5 $1 { Good timing; the battle between White's knight and Black's bishop is easily won. } cxd5 ( { Or } 22... exd5 23. Re5 Qf6 24. f4 b4 25. Rae1 { when the invasion down the e-file decides the game. } ) 
23. Rac1 Qc7 24. g4 $1 { Announcing a decisive kingside attack. } f6 { Preventing g4-g5. } ( { Instead } 24... b4 25. g5 h5 ( { Or } 25... hxg5 26. Qxg5 { followed by Kh1, Rg1 winning. } ) 
26. Qf4 Bc6 27. Re3 g6 28. Rf3 Qe7 29. Qe5 { then the win is imminent after Rf6 and one of many tactical shots, such as f2-f4-f5. } ) 
25. f4 b4 26. f5 $18 { Opening the e-file decisively. The rest is easy. } exf5 27. gxf5 Be8 28. Re3 Rd8 29. Rce1 Bc6 30. Qg2 Rd7 ( 30... Rxd6 $2 31. Re7 $1 { and wins. } ) 
31. Qg6 Qa7 32. Re8 Rxd6 33. R8e7 $1 { Final remarks 1. Throughout the entire game, White's d4-pawn was never vulnerable and the d5-square did not do much for Black, hence the c4-c5 move did not really weaken anything. 2. The move c4-c5 prevented essentially all of Black's attempts for counterplay. In particular, Black's light-squared bishop proved useless throughout the game. 3. 11...Be7 was a serious mistake, as it allowed White to seize control of the crucial d6-square, which was essential in White's victory. 4. Finally, this game serves as another example of White's positional advantage if the breaks ...c6-c5 and ...e6-e5 are impossible. Black's imprecise opening condemned him to this type of inferiority from the very beginning. } 
 1-0

