 { Conrad Holt - Gata Kamsky, Saint Louis 2013 Learning objective: This game is an example of Black's successful ...c6-c5 break followed by an attack against White's king. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 a6 5. e3 g6 6. h3 Bg7 7. Bd3 O-O 8. O-O e6 9. b3 Re8 10. Bb2 Nbd7 11. Qc2 b6 12. Rfd1 Bb7 13. e4 dxe4 14. Nxe4 Nxe4 15. Bxe4 { The key position has been reached; Black manages to free his position with the nice sequence: } f5 $1 16. Bd3 c5 { Black has solved his space problem and now White must play carefully to maintain equality. } 17. Be2 ( { The tempting } 17. d5 $6 Bxb2 { runs into: } 18. Qxb2 Qf6 $1 ( { But not } 18. Be2 exd5 $6 19. cxd5 Bxd5 20. Bxa6 { with a complicated game } ) 
19. Qxf6 Nxf6 { The d5-pawn is now pinned! } 20. dxe6 Bxf3 21. gxf3 Rxe6 $17 ) 
Qe7 18. dxc5 bxc5 ( { The alternative } 18... Nxc5 19. Bxg7 Qxg7 { is okay too, but it gives more mobility to White's queenside majority. } ) 
19. Bxg7 Qxg7 { We have reached a critical moment in the game. Black's plan is quite clear: he must advance his pawn majority on the kingside and possibly create some threats against the king. White must create counterplay by pushing his majority on the queenside, but this is not easy as after b3-b4 the c4-pawn will be vulnerable. Black's position is not necessarily superior from an objective perspective, but the reader should understand that conducting Black's side will be a much simpler task than White's. } 20. Qd2 Re7 21. b4 $1 { There are no other constructive plans. } e5 22. bxc5 Nxc5 { PH: It might seem like White is getting outplayed, since Black now has the better pawn structure and a nice blockade on c5, but it's not the case. White is bravely advancing on the queenside, and Black's minor pieces, while they are good pieces, they are also rather loose and unsafe. } 23. Qa5 Rc8 24. Rab1 ( { More accurate is } 24. Rd8 Rxd8 25. Qxd8 Qf8 26. Qa5 ( { Trading queens with } 26. Qxf8 $6 Kxf8 { leads to an unpleasant endgame due to Black's superior pawn structure, for example } 27. Rd1 Ke8 28. Nd2 Rd7 29. Nb3 Rxd1 30. Bxd1 Nd3 $15 { and only Black can play for a win } ) 
Re8 27. Nd2 Qe7 { When chances are approximately level. } ) 
Ree8 ( { More accurate is } 24... e4 $1 25. Nd4 ( { The endgame arising after } 24... Ree8 24. Rd8 Rxd8 26. Qxd8 Qf8 27. Qxf8 Kxf8 $15 { is uncomfortable for White } ) 
e3 $5 { with good attacking chances for Black. } ) 
25. Nd2 e4 26. Nb3 Ne6 { Of course Black does not wish to trade pieces yet, as it is difficult for White to create threats effectively. } 27. c5 { It is easy to see that this weak and isolated pawn will not get very far. } ( { Safer is } 27. Qd2 Qe5 { though Black has a comfortable game. } ) 
e3 $1 { Beginning to create threats against White's king. } 28. fxe3 Qe5 29. Qd2 Rcd8 ( { An option was } 29... Red8 $5 30. Qe1 Qxe3 31. Qf2 Qxf2 32. Kxf2 { with roughly level chances } ) 
 ( { But of course not } 29... Nxc5 $4 30. Nxc5 Qxc5 31. Rxb7 { and wins. } ) 
30. Qe1 Bd5 $5 { Black wants to keep the game sharp. } ( { The alternative } 30... Rxd1 31. Rxd1 Nxc5 { simplifies too much for Black's ambitions. } ) 
31. Bxa6 Ra8 32. Bb5 $2 { White miscalculates in a difficult position. } ( { Necessary was } 32. Bf1 Rxa2 { when Black enjoys good compensation for the pawn. PH: Actually after } 33. Nc1 $1 { followed by Nd3 White is just better. } ) 
Rxa2 33. Rxd5 $2 ( { If } 33. Nd2 Rea8 { Black has a very dangerous initiative } ) 
 ( { While } 33. Bxe8 $4 { loses immediately after } Rxg2 34. Kf1 Qh2 { with forced checkmate. } ) 
Qxd5 34. e4 ( 34. Qf1 Re7 $19 { doesn't help either. } ) 
fxe4 35. Bxe8 e3 $19 { This is probably what White missed when he decided to play 32. Bb5. } 36. Bf7+ $5 { A humorous resource. } ( { Black is winning after } 36. Qg3 Nf4 $1 { since } 37. Qxf4 $2 Qxg2 { is mate. } ) 
Kxf7 37. Qg3 Kg7 $1 { Preparing 38... Nf4. } ( { But of course not } 37... Nf4 $4 38. Qxf4 ) 
38. Nc1 Nf4 { Final remarks 1. The reader should remember Black's 18... bxc5. This move played a key role as it made it difficult for White to advance his queenside majority. Meanwhile, Black had a healthy kingside attack based on advancing his central majority. 2. Although White was objectively no worse until move 33, he lacked targets and his position was quite difficult on a practical level. The key to everything was White's lack of a healthy pawn majority since the passed c5-pawn was more of a weakness than a threat. } 
 0-1

