 { Vadim Zvjaginsev - Alexander Evdokimov, 64th Russian Championship, Taganrog 2011 Learning objective: This is an example of how Black may carry out the ...b7-b5 break, and how White can meet this break and turn the c6-pawn into a weakness. } 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. Nf3 Nd7 7. Bd3 e6 8. O-O Ngf6 9. b3 Be7 10. Bb2 O-O 11. c4 { White enjoys a small advantage as is usual in this type of structure. } Qa5 ( { PH: I'm not a huge fan of how Black plays here, and I would prefer either } 11... Qc7 { followed by putting the rooks on d8 and e8 or } a5 { intending ...a4 if allowed, and if } 12. a3 { then } Qb6 { looking at the pawn on b3. Black is completely fine. } ) 
