59. Rxg7 Rxa5 60. Rg6 Kd4 61. Rxh6 Kxe4 62. Rf6 $18 { The rest is simple for White as the g- and h-pawns are unstoppable. } Ra1 63. Kg2 Ra2+ 64. Rf2 Ra8 65. h6 Rh8 66. g5 Kd3 67. Rf7 Rg8 68. Rg7 { Final remarks 1. The reader should remember how threatening the ...b7-b5 break can be. The response cxb5 is often unsatisfactory as it gives up the d5-square permanently and makes White's queenside vulnerable. 2. Allowing Black to achieve the trades ...b5xc4 and b3xc4 enables Black to gain good counterplay along the b-file. 3. For this reason, the most memorable point in this game was Zvjaginsev's idea 15. Nf5 followed by 16. Ne3 and later recapturing with Nxc4. This turned the c6-pawn into a permanent weakness while preventing Black's further breaks with ...c6-c5 or ...a6-a5. } 
